### Step 6 — Review

By the core's rigor principle, review is one event at Step 6, once the code settles: a reading costs minutes whatever it finds, and cannot see across pieces until the head settles.

**The readers.** The rigor deals the three questions `evidence` · `adversarial` · `structure`: at `single` the critic holds `evidence`, `adversarial` and `structure`; at `double` the auditor holds `evidence` and the critic the other two. `structure`'s `reuse` and `one-site` findings are rated on the debt table. The auditor falsifies the *evidence* and never reviews the code; the critic falsifies the *code*. A reader is never the author nor a fork of the orchestrator, and is shown no other reader's verdict. Its brief names its questions on a line of its own, `Questions: <q>[, <q>]`, exactly the set the plan's rigor deals its role, or the dispatch wall refuses it; and nothing of their checks: `payload/context/checks-<question>.md` is delivered to it at start. It writes one record per question it is dealt, and its brief lists every record on `Files:`, `Expected artifact:` naming one of them. The reader dealt `evidence`, whichever role holds it, declares one to three runs in all, suites under `Suites:` and other runners under `Re-executes:`; `Suites: none` with no `Re-executes:` is for a reader not dealt `evidence`.

**The reading, once.** The readers are dispatched once, when the last build row has landed, in the one batch `steps/5.md` dispatches. Each reads the settled whole (`scope: whole`), the pieces together and never each piece again, and `proof-add review` registers the reading.

**The record and its fact.** Each record is a file under `record/` with flush-left lines `reviewed: <a>..<b>`, `question: <q>`, `result: <pass|flag|fail>`, `scope: <piece|whole>`; for `structure`, one line `check: <id> <PASS|FLAG|FAIL|n/a> <reason>` per check id. When the reader's roster row carries `severity` in `pushed=`, the record also holds the finding lines `payload/context/severity.md` sets out; it writes no priority, and one that does is refused when it differs from the table. Otherwise a record with no `findings:` line registers with `result:` as written. Register it with `session-poker.sh proof-add review <record> --question <q> --reader <name>`, `<record>` spelled `record/…` from the docs root or as an absolute path: the head is the record's `b`; the reader's role is read from its roster row, never typed. Code past a question's last head is uncovered for that question.

**A rating is final, and a move is the user's word.** `session-poker.sh finding-move <record>#<n> <defer|fix> '<the user's words>' '<why>'` moves a finding across the line, on the main thread only, and only on words the user typed in a prompt of this session: at least three whole words, or their whole prompt. A pasted block, a tool result and another agent's text are not theirs, and an S1 is never deferred. A record path registers one pass; the `deferred:` and `moved:` lines that bind a finding belong to that pass, so a later pass is written to a new record path.

**The disposition.** A finding is a triage item, never a task. Each follows the policy by severity, one line on the review results page, and is the user's decision only when it names an unproved criterion or you recommend against its default (`review-results --against`). Like every card a verb prints, the page is shown whole, in one fenced block. A row exists only from the user's ruling, attributed on the row; the one row you may make alone is an in-diff finding inside `fix-policy:` and under `fix-cap:`, marked `born: review S<n> <reach>`. A debt finding is never ruled: record it with `session-poker.sh debt add <record>`; it is burned when touched (`## Debt ledger` in `operational-rules.md`). Only readers rate; no agent moves a rating.

**Stop rule.** Three fixes on one component stop the run.

**The waiver is the user's.** `session-poker.sh waive <question> '<reply>'` writes `waived: question=<q> head=<40-hex> by <git user.name> <ISO-UTC> "<reason>"`, covering that question up to that head. An agent never waives.

**The order.** The readers dispatch when the code settles; the readings and their fix rows come first. At Step 7 the release row (`approval:plan, head`) lands, the user approves its head, then the regression row (`approval:release, head`) runs there. `approval:release` and `approval:merge` sit on the integrate row, each a decision on the review results page.
